Cut the per-EFB-copy multisample cost on tile-based GPUs.

WHAT COSTS WHAT. set_efb_targets() hangs a resolveTarget off every EFB render
pass and every GXCopyTex splits the pass (resolve_pass_into), so under MSAA a
copy carries two distinct costs. Measured, at file select, MSAA 4x, 1280x960:

  A) the full-attachment multisample resolve -- writes ONE 1x-sized texture
     (W*H*4 B) and, on a tiler, runs inside tile memory. 3.75 MiB/copy here.
  B) the pass break itself -- colorStoreOp/depthStoreOp both default to Store,
     so the MULTISAMPLED color AND depth attachments (W*H*samples*4 B each) are
     flushed to main memory, and the continuation pass's loadOp: Load drags
     them back in. 37.5 MiB stored + 37.5 MiB loaded per break at 4x.

(B) is an order of magnitude larger than (A) and is the term MSAA multiplies by
the sample count; (A) is sample-count-independent. (B) dominates, and on a
tile-based mobile GPU it is the frame. Per-frame aurora counters at file select
before this patch: passes=6 efbCopies=5 msaaResolves=6, msStore=225.00 MiB
msLoad=168.75 MiB -- 393.75 MiB/frame of multisampled attachment traffic, vs
112.50 MiB on a plain 2-pass screen.

The canonical mobile fix -- storeOp: Discard on the multisampled attachment --
is NOT generally legal here: rendering RESUMES into the same EFB after a copy,
so the samples are usually still needed. Two narrower cases are legal:

(1) BACK-TO-BACK COPIES. MP6's file select copies four 320x168 quadrants of one
    finished EFB image with no draw between them (framescope: four CopyTex at
    the same draw index). Each gets its own pass, and three of them are EMPTY --
    no draws, no clears. An empty pass's resolve output is byte-identical to the
    previous pass's, which already landed in the very same resolve target, so
    its whole BeginRenderPass/End is dead work that only buys a store+reload of
    both multisampled attachments. Skip it and encode the copy alone against the
    still-current resolve target. Tracked by two new RenderPass flags:
    msResolveCurrent (set ONLY on the continuation pass resolve_pass_into
    creates, whose predecessor always carries a resolveTarget and therefore
    always resolves) and skipRenderPass. Any other pass-creating path
    (begin_frame, begin_color_pass, begin/end_offscreen, resume_efb_pass_loading)
    leaves msResolveCurrent false, so the skip cannot escape a run of
    consecutive GXCopyTex calls on an otherwise untouched EFB.

(2) THE CONTINUATION CLEARS. GXCopyTex(dest, GX_TRUE) gives the next pass
    loadOp: Clear, so the samples this pass would store are overwritten before
    anything reads them -- Discard them. WebGPU writes resolveTarget
    independently of storeOp, so the copy is unaffected. Excluded when the pass
    also feeds captureDepthSnapshot / a resolve_pass depth snapshot.

Both are gated on msaaSamples > 1: with MSAA off nothing in this file behaves
differently (verified -- identical per-frame counters with the change compiled
in and compiled out).

Verified: same counters after -- passes=6 skipped=3 efbCopies=5 msaaResolves=3,
msStore=93.75 MiB msLoad=56.25 MiB, i.e. 393.75 -> 150.00 MiB/frame (-61.9%) and
6 -> 3 resolves, with the 5 EFB copies all still issued. Plain screens are
untouched (2 passes, 0 skipped, identical bytes). Shadow-map dumps at file
select under MSAA 4x are unchanged within run-to-run noise (mean silhouette
coverage 12.84% before, 12.84% after, 12.85% for a same-setting control run;
before-vs-after pixel delta ~55k px over 27 dumps vs ~54k px for the control).

This is a BANDWIDTH win. It cannot be observed on a desktop discrete GPU, which
is CPU-bound on this workload, and no desktop framerate improvement is claimed.

